In article <2uc6nk$t16@rc1.vub.ac.be>, Karel Driesen <kjdriese@vnet3.vub.ac.be> writes:
>Don't get too exited seeing the title of this post. A friend of mine 
>has started playing from a save file of mine (on a Mac, you can save),
>and it seemed a good idea to show off the difference of good and bad
>playing style by both playing the same character in the same dungeon.
>
>The problem is that this doesn't work, since every level is generated 
>only when you enter it. If the implementors make it possible to generate
>all levels in advance, you have exactly the same playing conditions,
>and you can start to organise competition games (just for bragging rights,
>since cheating is far too easy). Of course there would still be an
>element of chance, but that makes it interesting (even by taking the
>same initial seed for the random generator, one different action anywhere
>would give you a different outcome for all subsequent actions).
>
>So how does this sound ? You could even make some "classical" dungeons
>ftp-able, or some thematic dungeons (a shop on every level, or no shops
>at all, or a dungeon without any potions, or a dungeon with all live
>creatures genocided, so you have only undeads stalking around)

I really like this idea.  An initially seeded random number would control
the layout of the dungeon, and maybe even the generation of monsters and 
items.  It wouldn't control other things (like the initial randomizing 
of the identities, or the chances of hitting/missing, which would still
be "random").

I can hear the discussions now:
  - You should try playing 62987!  It has the biggest shop I'd ever
    seen on level 2, and an even bigger one on level 3, and three
    differently aligned temples on level 3...
  - Game 2435 has no random alters or shops in the entire game!
  - Try playing game 837 as a Tourist.  It's *impossible* to even
    get off level 1!

To do this, I guess you'd need separate random number functions which
would be used for things controlled by the initial random seed (which
would use the standard random number routines if you didn't use a
seed).
